# RISC0 Development Mode Standards

## Context
- Applied when working with RISC0 v1.2.1 in development mode
- Focuses on handling dev mode receipts and proofs
- Ensures consistent behavior between dev and production modes
- See 2001-risc0-api for high-level RISC0 integration guidelines
- See 2003-risc0-receipts for general receipt handling

## Requirements
- Handle dev mode receipt variants:
  - Check for `InnerReceipt::Fake` variant in dev mode
  - Access journal data normally
  - Handle claims without cryptographic verification
  - Expect minimal or empty seal data
  - Log appropriate dev mode warnings
- Follow dev mode patterns:
  - No actual proof generation
  - Journal data works normally
  - Claims exist but aren't verified
  - Seal operations are mocked
  - Zero-knowledge properties not enforced
- Implement proper dev mode detection:
  - Use `cfg!(feature = "dev-mode")` for compile-time checks
  - Log clear warnings about dev mode usage
  - Never use dev mode receipts in production
  - Handle both dev and production paths
- Emit appropriate metrics:
  - Log dev mode status
  - Track mock proof operations
  - Record journal sizes
  - Note absence of real proofs

## Examples

<example>
// Good: Complete dev mode receipt handling
fn handle_receipt(receipt: &Receipt) -> Result<()> {
    match &receipt.inner {
        InnerReceipt::Fake(_) => {
            debug!("Dev mode receipt detected");
            // Handle journal normally
            let journal = &receipt.journal;
            
            // Claims work but aren't verified
            if let Ok(claim) = receipt.claim() {
                debug!("Dev mode claim digest: {:?}", claim.digest());
            }
            
            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProofGeneration,
                mode => "dev",
                journal_size => journal.bytes.len()
            );
        }
        InnerReceipt::Composite(_) | InnerReceipt::Succinct(_) => {
            debug!("Production mode receipt");
            // Normal proof handling
        }
        _ => {
            error!("Unexpected receipt type");
            return Err(anyhow::anyhow!("Invalid receipt type"));
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}
</example>

<example>
// Good: Dev mode metrics
fn emit_dev_metrics(receipt: &Receipt) {
    match &receipt.inner {
        InnerReceipt::Fake(_) => {
            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProofGeneration,
                mode => "dev",
                warning => "no_cryptographic_verification"
            );
            
            emit_histogram!(MetricEvents::JournalSize,
                receipt.journal.bytes.len() as f64,
                mode => "dev"
            );
        }
        _ => {
            emit_event!(MetricEvents::ProofGeneration,
                mode => "production"
            );
        }
    }
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Missing dev mode handling
fn process_receipt(receipt: &Receipt) {
    match &receipt.inner {
        InnerReceipt::Composite(_) | InnerReceipt::Succinct(_) => {
            // Only handles production receipts
        }
        _ => panic!("Unknown receipt type"), // Wrong: should handle Fake
    }
}
</example>

<example type="invalid">
// Bad: Using dev mode receipts in production
fn verify_proof(receipt: &Receipt) -> bool {
    match &receipt.inner {
        InnerReceipt::Fake(_) => true, // NEVER accept fake receipts in production
        _ => verify_real_proof(receipt),
    }
}
</example>

## Critical Notes
<critical>
- NEVER use dev mode receipts in production
- ALWAYS handle InnerReceipt::Fake variant
- Log clear warnings about dev mode usage
- Maintain consistent journal handling
- Track dev mode in metrics
- Document dev mode limitations
- Handle both modes cleanly
</critical> 